      <p>Local service accounts fail in a particular way. The reporting says calls are up and cost per lead is down, while the business says the phone is ringing with suppliers, recruiters and people who wanted somebody forty miles away.</p>
      <p>That gap is almost always the conversion definition. Fix what counts as a lead and the whole account changes.</p>

      <h2>Call tracking that means something</h2>
      <p>The default setup counts any call over a set duration as a conversion. Fifteen seconds is long enough for a wrong number to count.</p>
      <p>So Smart Bidding learns to buy calls, not customers. It finds the searches that make phones ring cheaply, which are rarely the searches that make people book.</p>
      <p>What we do instead:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Call conversions qualified against the booking system or CRM, not against a timer</li>
        <li>Offline conversion imports, so a job that closed three days later is fed back to the campaign that produced it</li>
        <li>Separate conversion actions for enquiry and booked job, valued differently</li>
        <li>Call reporting kept distinct from form fills, because they behave differently and often come from different searches</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The first month of accurate data usually shows cost per booked job is well above what was being reported. That is uncomfortable and it is the number the business can actually act on.</p>

      <h2>Local Services Ads</h2>
      <p>Local Services Ads sit above everything else in the results and charge per lead rather than per click, which changes how the whole account is run.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Setup and the Google verification process, including licence and insurance checks</li>
        <li>Google Business Profile linked and kept consistent</li>
        <li>Service and area selection, which decides lead quality more than the budget does</li>
        <li>Lead disputes filed for calls that were not genuine enquiries, because unchallenged bad leads raise your cost per job and train the system badly</li>
        <li>Review generation prompts, since ranking in Local Services Ads is tied to reviews and responsiveness</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Local Services Ads and Search are not alternatives. They occupy different parts of the page and are usually run together, measured separately.</p>

      <h2>Geography is a lever, not a setting</h2>
      <p>Most local accounts use one radius around one pin, which treats a job twenty minutes away the same as one an hour away.</p>
      <p>Travel time, not distance, decides whether a job is profitable. We split locations by how far crews will actually go, bid differently by area, and exclude the places where the drive eats the margin. Where demand varies by suburb, the structure reflects that rather than averaging it.</p>
      <p>The location targeting setting also matters. The default includes people merely interested in your area, which for a plumber means paying for someone researching from another country.</p>

      <h2>Emergency and scheduled work</h2>
      <p>These are different businesses sharing one account. Someone searching "emergency boiler repair" at eleven at night converts at a different rate, at a different price, than someone searching "boiler service" on a Tuesday afternoon.</p>
      <p>Splitting urgent from planned demand lets each be bid and scheduled properly, including ad scheduling that reflects when the phone is actually answered. Paying for calls when nobody picks up is the most avoidable waste in local accounts.</p>

      <h2>Who this suits</h2>
      <p>HVAC, plumbing, electrical, landscaping, moving, cleaning, pest control, roofing, and any service business where the sale happens on a call and the job happens in person. We currently run accounts in several of these, which you can see on the <a href="/#clients">sectors list</a>.</p>

